proxmod (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  proxmod can now run inside qm, pct and pvesh, if an operator asks for it.

  A seam wrap installed in pvedaemon does not apply to a command somebody types.
  The three CLIs are eight-line stubs that load PVE::CLI::<tool> and dispatch in
  their own process, to the same PVE::API2 classes the daemons use — so the seam
  is there and only proxmod is missing. Found on a live host, where an
  over-quota create was refused through the REST API and succeeded as
  `qm create`, same node and same second. Recorded as [PVE-F-055].

  Three patch specs in patches/ insert `use Proxmod;` into the three PVE::CLI
  modules. They ship DISABLED, and a default install patches nothing and behaves
  exactly as before. Enabling one gives up `dpkg -V` on that file, which is the
  strongest claim proxmod makes, and puts proxmod in the path of the primary
  tool for managing guests — so it is the operator's decision and ADR 0013
  explains what they are taking on. The binaries in /usr/sbin were never
  candidates: reaching them would have meant adding /usr/sbin and /usr/bin to
  the patch allowlist, which is a far worse trade than the patch. PERL5OPT was
  not a candidate either — taint mode ignores it, and pct is -T while qm and
  pvesh are not, so it would have enforced on VMs and silently not on
  containers.

  Proxmod::Registry gains a notion of a known CLI, kept separate from a known
  daemon because the difference is consulted at three sites and each needs it. A
  CLI has a real terminal, so its output stays there; it renders no pages, so
  the frontend stage is skipped; it is transient, so nothing durable is recorded
  from it. An extension has to name a CLI in backend.daemons to be loaded into
  one, and the default when that key is absent stays daemons-only — an extension
  written before this existed must not start running inside `qm` on upgrade.

  Proxmod::API::scope_available lets an extension probe a scope instead of
  finding out by dying. qm and pct do not load PVE::API2::Cluster, and because
  an extension registers its routes and installs its seam wraps in one call, a
  mount that died there would take the wraps with it — which are the whole
  reason for being in a CLI. pvesh does build the full tree, so it can now see
  proxmod's own endpoints, which it never could before.

  Also fixes the syslog switch introduced in 0.3.1, which was set before proxmod
  knew what it was running inside. It is now set for a detaching daemon and
  nothing else, so `perl -MProxmod -e1` and a patched `qm` both keep the
  terminal they were run from.

 -- Matthew Mckenzie <matthew.m.mckenzie@gmail.com>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 10:00:00 +0000

proxmod (0.3.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  Everything logged after boot went to /dev/null.

  Proxmod::Log wrote to stderr on the stated grounds that "systemd wires both
  daemons' output to the journal, so STDERR *is* the log". That is true only
  during the INIT phase. PVE::Daemon reopens STDOUT on /dev/null and then STDERR
  onto STDOUT once the daemon detaches (Daemon.pm:313-337), so both are
  /dev/null for the master and every worker it forks — confirmed on a live host,
  where /proc/<pid>/fd/2 for a pvedaemon worker points at /dev/null.

  So registration lines appeared and nothing else ever did. The failure is
  invisible in exactly the places you would look for it: a test harness
  localises $FH and sees the output, proxmodctl and proxmod-verify have a real
  stderr and see it too, and proxmod-verify's own journal grep only ever checks
  for boot lines. It cost proxmod-pool-quota every refusal log line in
  production — the wrap fired, the caller got a 403 naming the limit, and
  `journalctl -u pvedaemon` said nothing at all.

  Inside a daemon the line now goes to syslog, which PVE has already opened with
  the right tag and facility (Daemon.pm:244 calls initlog), and which lands in
  the same journal an administrator was already reading. Proxmod::Boot sets the
  switch; proxmodctl and proxmod-verify do not, so they keep the terminal they
  were run from. The message is passed as an argument to a '%s' format and never
  as the format itself, because these lines carry text an operator typed.

  t/00-compile.t pins all of it, including that a stderr nobody is reading stays
  empty.

 -- Matthew Mckenzie <matthew.m.mckenzie@gmail.com>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

proxmod (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  Wrapping a Proxmox seam is now something proxmod does, not something every
  extension re-derives.

  conventions.md has always said "probe before you wrap; a seam that moved
  should produce a missing feature and a log line, never a stuck interface".
  That was prose with no code behind it. The only implementation was
  Frontend::_wrap — private, specific to two seams of PVE::Service::pveproxy,
  and fail-open — so the first backend extension to enforce anything wrote the
  whole thing again: the probe, the eval, the log-once rule, idempotency, and a
  ledger to report what was live. The next one would have written a third.

  Proxmod::API gains wrap_method and wrap_sub. wrap_method replaces
  $info->{code} on the live method hashref and nothing else, which covers both
  ways PVE reaches a method — handle($info, ...) and the class method AUTOLOAD
  installs lazily, closing over the same hashref [PVE-F-054], newly harvested.
  wrap_sub does the same for a plain named function. A seam that is not found is
  logged once, left unwrapped, and recorded with the reason; it does not throw
  and does not stop the other seams installing.

  posture is a required argument with no default, taking closed or open. This is
  ADR 0006's argument about permissions applied to a second silent default: a
  hook that dies either refuses the wrapped call or is swallowed, those are
  opposites, and guessing wrong is invisible in one direction and takes out the
  web interface in the other. proxmod has two consumers and they split one
  apiece, so there is no majority to default to. ADR 0012 records it.

  Proxmod::Frontend now uses the shared facility instead of its own copy. Its
  two seams appear in Proxmod::API::seams() and therefore in proxmod-verify for
  the first time — previously a moved get_index was one log line at boot and
  nothing afterwards, which is no use to an administrator asking six months
  later. t/06-frontend.t passes unchanged, which is the evidence the refactor
  moved no behaviour.

  proxmod-verify reports which seams are wrapped and which are not, beside the
  route replay it already does.

  Separately, the booted line's extensions= figure counted stages, not
  extensions. An extension declaring both a frontend and a backend half is one
  extension and pveproxy runs both halves, so boot() summed two stage results as
  if they were disjoint sets and reported it twice — which proxmod-verify then
  rendered to an administrator as "2 extension(s) loaded". Frontend::install and
  Backend::install now return the ids they loaded and failed rather than counts,
  and boot() unions them, so each applicable extension is counted exactly once:
  in extensions= only if every stage it was applicable to succeeded, otherwise
  in failed=. The two now partition the applicable set. An extension whose
  backend registered but whose frontend was dropped counts once, as failed,
  because the question behind failed= is whether anything needs looking at. The
  line's shape is untouched, so proxmod-verify's parser and daemons started by
  an older proxmod are unaffected. specifications.md gains REQ-FW-018b, whose
  absence is why the test suite asserted the wrong number as correct.

  Note for upgrades: registry= folds proxmod's own version into the fingerprint,
  so each node reports drift against the others until every one of them is on
  0.3.0. That is expected, and it clears as the rollout completes.

 -- Matthew Mckenzie <matthew.m.mckenzie@gmail.com>  Fri, 28 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

proxmod (0.2.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * proxmodctl list printed nothing. The reader expected `id = value` and the
    manifests are JSON, so every extension on the host parsed to the empty
    string and the command reported success over an empty list. doctor showed
    an empty extensions section for the same reason, and because it had already
    set found=1 it could not even say so. Both now delegate to
    Proxmod::Registry, which is the parser the daemons use, so the list a
    human reads and the registry a daemon loads can no longer disagree.
  * Corrupt patch state is no longer destroyed by the next write. read_state
    already returned a broken flag on unparseable state; nothing consumed it,
    and write_state re-encoded only version and patches, so apply() would read
    a corrupt file, get an empty hash, add one entry and write it — taking
    every prior orig_sha256 and backup path with it, which is precisely the
    orphaned-backup failure the module exists to prevent. apply, converge and
    revert_all now refuse to write over broken state and say so.
  * The exec wrapper's guarded-path list omitted both patch directories. It
    covered extensions.d on each root but not /usr/share/proxmod/patches or
    /etc/proxmod/patches, and the walk is -maxdepth 1, so the directories were
    checked and the spec files inside them were not. A world-writable spec
    under either could rewrite a Proxmox module as root inside pvedaemon while
    the wrapper called the tree safe.
  * proxmod-verify's structure check resolves routes now. It carried a ten-line
    banner promising to catch an endpoint that registered and is nonetheless
    unreachable because it was mounted under a greedy path parameter, and its
    body counted extensions and returned. Proxmod::API::assert_route existed
    for this and was called only by a test named for the question verify asks.
  * libhttp-message-perl is a direct Depends. Frontend requires it at runtime
    and reached it only through libpve-http-server-perl; CI installing it by
    hand was the tell.
  * proxmodctl logs -f no longer appears to hang, having piped into grep
    without --line-buffered. proxmod-reapply's restart decision anchors to
    the child's changed= field rather than matching the substring anywhere in
    its output, and its health check no longer counts activating as healthy,
    which is how a slow-failing daemon reached the end of convergence without
    ever tripping panic_unwrap.
  * The package ships the documentation it already promised. proxmod.conf is a
    conffile telling every administrator to read proxmodctl(8) and
    /usr/share/doc/proxmod/extension-manifest.md, and proxmod-verify.service
    declared Documentation= for a man page that did not exist. Both man pages
    and the operator-facing docs are installed now.
  * LICENSE, CONTRIBUTING.md and SECURITY.md at the paths GitHub reads, and a
    maintainer address that is a real one.
  * AGENTS.md, as a pointer to docs/conventions.md rather than a second copy of
    it. A contributor or agent arriving cold got no pointer to the no-strict
    rule, the /etc/pve prohibition or the fact ledger, all of which are law.
  * t/11-conventions.t and t/13-invariants.t assert mechanically what the
    conventions asserted only in prose: the four-line status block on every
    doc, every [PVE-F-nnn] citation resolving to the ledger and every ledger
    entry being cited, the version fan-out agreeing everywhere it is stated,
    install lines naming no version they will outlive, and the deliberate
    duplications at the taint boundaries staying in step. The last of those had
    already drifted: Frontend's copy of _read_file returned undef for a
    zero-byte file where Registry's and Patch's return '', which is invisible
    until loader_body dies on an empty template with a stale errno.
  * The release path runs the tests it publishes from, lintian is a gate rather
    than a comment on both paths, and make lint fails instead of passing when
    a linter is missing and LINT_STRICT is set. Actions are pinned by digest,
    ci.yml has a concurrency group, dependabot watches the base image, and a
    monthly job runs the PVE source harvest so a moved seam is reported by a
    machine rather than noticed by whoever next reads the sentence it broke.
  * Releases are published from debian/changelog rather than from generated
    commit subjects, and additionally as an OCI artifact on ghcr.io. A release
    tag must be annotated and its message must carry an E2E: line naming the
    QEMU run it was cut from, or saying why there is none.
  * test/integration/12-patch.sh covers the patch facility against a real
    index.html.tpl across a real pve-manager upgrade, which is the claim
    Patch.pm exists to make and had been proven only against stubs.
  * docs/compatibility.md states what is and is not claimed for a cluster,
    including that no automated test covers any of it.

 -- proxmod maintainers <proxmod@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 27 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

proxmod (0.2.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Frontend: proxmod-ui.js no longer runs in strict mode. ExtJS resolves
    callParent by reading Function.caller on the calling method, and V8 hands
    out null for that whenever the caller is a strict-mode function, so every
    callParent under the directive failed inside ext-all.js with "Cannot read
    properties of null (reading 'apply')". The override proxmod installs on
    PVE.dc.Config and its seven siblings calls its parent first by design, so
    the config panel never finished building and took the left-hand menu tree
    with it. The override is installed on first registration, which is why a
    host with no extension looked healthy and the interface broke as soon as
    one arrived. Strictness is inherited by nested functions, so the directive
    is gone from the file rather than from one method.
  * The shipped example extension carried the same defect and is fixed with it.
  * Guarded against a repeat, twice, because node --check cannot see it: make
    lint-js rejects any asset that combines a 'use strict' directive with
    callParent or callSuper, and t/06-frontend.t asserts the same over the
    files this repo ships.
  * Docs: the frontend authoring guide taught the bug — its copy-paste skeleton
    carried the directive and its checklist required it. Corrected there and in
    conventions, js-api and pve-internals, with the failure written out in
    troubleshooting under the error text a browser prints.

 -- proxmod maintainers <proxmod@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 22 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

proxmod (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Frontend: extensions can own an item in a config panel's left-hand menu
    tree, not only a tab. Proxmod.ui gains addMenuScreen(), addMenuSection(),
    addMenuItem() and configureMenu(), and the targets grow from four classes
    to eight (datacenter, node, qemu, lxc, storage, pool, zone, network).
    This is a new public JavaScript API, which is why this is a minor release
    rather than a patch.
  * proxmod-verify: a host with only backend extensions installed is healthy.
    It previously failed the http.index check because no extension asked for
    a loader tag, so the index correctly carried none (GH#1).
  * proxmod-reapply: restart the wrapped daemons when they are running an
    extension registry that is no longer the one on disk. Installing an
    extension package did not make it live, removing one did not make it go
    away, and upgrading proxmod itself left the old Perl modules resident in
    daemons nobody had restarted. Each daemon now logs a fingerprint of the
    registry it loaded and proxmod-verify recomputes it; the restart is
    bounded by a loop-breaker so a host that cannot load the new registry
    converges once and then reports it rather than restarting on every apt
    run. See docs/adr/0011-registry-fingerprint.md.
  * proxmod-verify: new --registry-only flag, as narrow as --live-only and
    deliberately separate from it. Exits 0 up to date, 1 out of date, 2 could
    not tell, printing the on-disk fingerprint on stdout.
  * Docs: the Proxmox VE sources are vendored as SHA-pinned, research-only
    submodules under docs/third_party/, so claims about unofficial seams can
    be checked against the source rather than remembered. They are excluded
    from the source package.
  * Tests: new integration test 11-registry, which removes and reinstalls the
    example extension with no --force anywhere; the purge test moves to 12.

 -- proxmod maintainers <proxmod@users.noreply.github.com>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

proxmod (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release.
  * Runtime injection of extensions into pvedaemon and pveproxy via a
    fail-safe systemd ExecStart wrapper. No Proxmox-owned file is modified.
  * Backend extension API (Proxmod::API) with idempotent registration and a
    post-registration check that the route actually resolves.
  * Frontend injection of a single loader script tag at index render time,
    plus Proxmod.ui/Proxmod.api for extension authors.
  * dpkg trigger driven convergence (proxmod-reapply), so an extension package
    needs no maintainer scripts and an upgrade needs nothing reapplied.

 -- proxmod maintainers <proxmod@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
